  "slug": "clinical-genomic-9867",
  "summary": "Single-cell, spatial and bulk transcriptomic profiling is now linking tumor cell states to prognosis and therapy response across glioma and sarcoma.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of transcriptomic features to define tumor cell states and prognosis. Several items apply single-cell, snRNA-seq or spatial transcriptomics to glioma: a 69-tumor atlas links IDH status to malignant and immune architecture, a reduced 366-gene Xenium panel derived from Neftel glioblastoma states retains about 80% concordance, and a 40-patient study identifies a mesenchymal recurrence subtype linked to a wound-healing surgical margin. Bulk-derived prognostic models appear for HBQ1 in glioma, PSEN1 in SHH medulloblastoma, and a six-gene entosis-related index in lung adenocarcinoma validated across TCGA and GEO. Convergent high-risk states in rhabdomyosarcoma and CD74-positive antigen-presenting glioma cells extend this to cell-state resolution. Functional validation is confined to selected items, including FEN1 inhibition synergizing with temozolomide in glioblastoma stem cells and STAG2 depletion accelerating NPM1::ALK transformation. What is new is the joint use of spatial and single-cell resolution with prognostic classification, while several items remain largely descriptive.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-07"
}
